Fixes: 969bf05eb3ce ("bpf: direct packet access")
Signed-off-by: Alexei Starovoitov <ast@kernel.org>
Acked-by: Daniel Borkmann <daniel@iogearbox.net>
Signed-off-by: David S. Miller <davem@davemloft.net>

Here is the root cause:
The bug arises from the BPF verifier's inability to correctly handle packet header accesses made in decreasing order. Specifically, when a program accesses packet data in a non-linear fashion (e.g., reading TCP ports before IP source addresses), the verifier fails to recognize valid accesses that occur within the safe range, leading to potential false negatives in access validation. This oversight can result in invalid memory access errors when the program attempts to read data that the verifier incorrectly deems out of bounds.

Here is the patch:

 static int check_packet_access(struct verifier_env *env, u32 regno, int off,
 			       int size)
 {
 	struct reg_state *regs = env->cur_state.regs;
 	struct reg_state *reg = &regs[regno];
-	int linear_size = (int) reg->range - (int) reg->off;

-	if (linear_size < 0 || linear_size >= MAX_PACKET_OFF) {
-		verbose("verifier bug\n");
-		return -EFAULT;
-	}
-	if (off < 0 || off + size > linear_size) {
-		verbose("invalid access to packet, off=%d size=%d, allowed=%d\n",
-			off, size, linear_size);
+	off += reg->off;
+	if (off < 0 || off + size > reg->range) {
+		verbose("invalid access to packet, off=%d size=%d, R%d(id=%d,off=%d,r=%d)\n",
+			off, size, regno, reg->id, reg->off, reg->range);
 		return -EACCES;
 	}
 	return 0;
 }



